In the project, I have read and studied about some basics of RL and applied it in some assignments. Some basic libraries of Python like numpy and matplotlib were used in these assignments.
In the First week, I explored the N-armed bandit problem, focusing on the exploration–exploitation trade-off.
I implemented greedy, ε-greedy, optimistic greedy, and UCB strategies.
Greedy exploits current knowledge but often converges to suboptimal actions.
ε-greedy and optimistic greedy improve exploration and long-term reward.
UCB balances reward and uncertainty effectively and showed the best overall performance. 
The second week involved studying Markov Decision Processes (MDPs) and the Bellman Optimality Equation.
MDPs were formulated by defining states, actions, transitions, and rewards.
I developed a mdp of slippery tiles and frozen lake enviroments, which involved stoichastic movements.
A key-based grid world was designed where reaching the goal required first acquiring a key.
The Bellman optimality equations were used to compute optimal value functions and policies.
These concepts were further applied to model and solve the Lights Out game as an MDP.
The experiments demonstrated how dynamic programming enables optimal decision-making in sequential problems.